Q. Why don't we send Entity directly?

Answer:
Entity represents the database table, whereas DTO represents the data exchanged between the client and the server.
 Using DTOs prevents exposing database fields, improves security, and allows independent changes to the API and database.

 Client
    ↓
 UserRequestDTO
    ↓
 Controller
    ↓
 Service
    ↓
 Entity
    ↓
 Repository

 Notice:

 Client never sends Entity.
 Client sends DTO.
 Service converts DTO → Entity.






 📄 Validation (DTO)
 Purpose
 Validates incoming request data before it reaches the Service layer.
 Prevents invalid data from being saved in the database.
 Dependency
 spring-boot-starter-validation
 Annotations
 @Valid

 Purpose

 Triggers validation on the request body.

 Example

 public ResponseEntity<User> createUser(
         @Valid @RequestBody UserRequestDTO dto)
 @NotBlank
 Field cannot be null, empty (""), or contain only spaces.
 @NotBlank(message = "Name is required")
 private String name;
 @NotNull
 Field cannot be null.
 @NotNull(message = "Age is required")
 private Integer age;
 @Email

 Checks if the email format is valid.

 @Email(message = "Invalid Email")
 @Min

 Minimum allowed value.

 @Min(value = 18, message = "Age must be greater than 18")
